name verbatlas-frame-info
# A frame on each row: its id (va:0001f, the key VerbAtlas points at the frame with), its name (TOLERATE, what the
# frame is, the type the highway lists), its definition, its prototypical BabelNet synset (a key of a
# WordNet synset, read as the ILI concept), that synset's definition, and a small definition.
# The first line is the release's banner (name, version, licence), not a row.
# README.txt: "Each line contains the ID and the name of a VerbAtlas frame ... we also provide a definition, a
# prototypical synset ..., the definition of the prototypical synset, and a "small definition" of the frame." The
# fields are named in those words.
match VA_frame_info.tsv

tier row by \n
skip 1
names ID name definition "prototypical synset" "definition of the prototypical synset" "small definition"
tier field by \t

thing row name
key row ID
type "prototypical synset" ili
content name definition "definition of the prototypical synset" "small definition"
# What each column means (Laplace-Engine#32), in the README's words: "From VerbAtlas 1.1.0, we also provide a
# definition, a prototypical synset (i.e., the synset that roughly represents the frame), the definition of the
# prototypical synset, and a "small definition" of the frame."; its changelog: "Added frame definitions and small
# definitions." The definition of the prototypical synset is said of that synset (the concept), not of the frame.
relation "frame definition" definition
relation "the synset that roughly represents the frame" "prototypical synset"
relation "small definition of the frame" "small definition"
relation "definition" "definition of the prototypical synset"
attest row definition "prototypical synset" "small definition"
attest row "definition of the prototypical synset" of "prototypical synset"

# the highway: a frame is its name (TOLERATE), and its id (va:0001f) is VerbAtlas's key of it
types vaframe "VerbAtlas frame" row
keyed vaframe row ID
